The Security Council,

Recalling its previous resolutions, statements of its President, and press statements concerning the situation in South Sudan, in particular resolutions 2625 (2022), 2677 (2023), 2729 (2024), and 2778 (2025),

Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, territorial integrity, and national unity of the Republic of South Sudan,

Recognizing the continued importance of the Revitalised Agreement on the Resolution of the Conflict in the Republic of South Sudan (R-ARCSS) as the foundational framework for peace and stability, and expressing deep concern over the persistent delays in its full and inclusive implementation,

Stressing that the Revitalised Transitional Government of National Unity (RTGoNU) bears the primary responsibility to protect its population from genocide, war crimes, ethnic cleansing, and crimes against humanity,

Expressing grave concern regarding the ongoing political stalemate, subnational and intercommunal violence, human rights violations and abuses, including conflict-related sexual and gender-based violence, violations of international humanitarian law, and attacks on humanitarian personnel and assets,

Expressing deep concern at the dire humanitarian crisis across South Sudan, compounded by severe food insecurity, climate-related shocks, economic deterioration, and the influx of hundreds of thousands of returnees and refugees fleeing the conflict in the Sudan,

Commending the vital role played by the United Nations Mission in the Republic of South Sudan (UNMISS) and United Nations agencies, funds, and programmes in maintaining peace, protecting civilians, and providing lifesaving assistance,

Determining that the situation in South Sudan continues to constitute a threat to international peace and security in the region,

Acting under Chapter VII of the Charter of the United Nations,

1. Decides to extend the mandate of UNMISS until 30 April 2026;

2. Decides that UNMISS’s mandate shall maintain an overall force ceiling of 17,000 troops and 2,101 police personnel, including 88 corrections officers, and expresses its readiness to consider adjustments to UNMISS’s force ceiling and mandate based on conditions on the ground;

3. Authorizes UNMISS to use all necessary means to perform the following core mandate tasks:
(a) Protection of civilians, through proactive, robust, and mobile deployment, the establishment of temporary operating bases, deterrence of violence against civilians, especially women and children, and support for community-level dialogue and conflict resolution;
(b) Creating the conditions conducive to the delivery of humanitarian assistance, including by ensuring the safety and security of humanitarian personnel, infrastructure, and supply routes, and monitoring and assisting the return, reintegration, or relocation of internally displaced persons;
(c) Supporting the implementation of the Revitalised Agreement and the peace process, including by offering good offices, advice, and technical support to the electoral process, constitution-drafting process, and transitional justice mechanisms, consistent with the aspirations of the South Sudanese people and international standards;
(d) Monitoring, investigating, and reporting on violations of international humanitarian law and violations and abuses of human rights, including by public reporting and supporting accountability mechanisms;

4. Demands that all parties to the conflict and armed groups immediately cease all hostilities, violence, and human rights violations throughout the country;

5. Urges the RTGoNU to demonstrate political will by expediting the implementation of key outstanding tasks of the R-ARCSS, including the deployment and operationalization of the Necessary Unified Forces, the completion of the permanent constitution-making process, and creating the necessary political, legal, and operational environment for free, fair, credible, and inclusive elections;

6. Condemns in the strongest terms all attacks, threats, and harassment directed against UNMISS peacekeepers, UN premises, and humanitarian personnel and assets, and demands that the RTGoNU and all other actors respect the Status of Forces Agreement (SOFA), ensure the freedom of movement of UNMISS personnel, and grant full, safe, rapid, and unhindered humanitarian access;

7. Welcomes the continued engagement and mediation efforts of the Intergovernmental Authority on Development (IGAD), the African Union, and other regional initiatives, including the high-level peace talks in Nairobi (Tumaini Initiative), and urges all stakeholders to align these efforts to consolidate peace;

8. Requests the Secretary-General to report to the Security Council every 90 days on the implementation of UNMISS’s mandate and the political and security situation in South Sudan;

9. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

